    <h3 class="mgdb-leaf-heading mgdb-spaced" id="gene-xref">Gene model cross-references and pan-genes</h3>
    <div class="gene-panel">
      <p>Please note that there is not a 1-to-1 correspondence between all gene models in all annotations. Some gene models are unique to specific genome assemblies, some have been split or merged between annotation or assembly versions, some direct associations may be difficult to calculate, so multiple gene models that are similar in sequence and position may be listed. And some gene models may be similar in sequence but do not appear in the same syntenic locations.</p>
      <details class="gene-details">
        <summary>Click to learn more.</summary>
        <p>The rules for associating gene models across assemblies require not only close sequence similarity, but also that they both appear in syntenic positions. Because of this, there are gene models that are not associated with all gene models in the current representative assembly annotation in spite of exact or near exact sequence similarity. This is often due to unplaced scaffolds that contain gene models.</p>
        <p>There are multiple ways to define and generate a pan-genome. Here we use "pan-gene" to mean to an analysis of multiple genome annotations to group gene models by sequence similarity and syntenic position, to identify core gene models \(present in all annotations\) and to identify orphan gene models \(present in only one\).</p>
      </details>
      <ul class="gene-link-list">
        <li><a href="https://download.maizegdb.org/Pan-genes/B73_gene_xref/">Gene model associations across all B73 assemblies</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="https://download.maizegdb.org/Pan-genes/Pan-Zea">Current pan-gene data</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

